  "mechanismSummary": "Methoxide removes the N–H proton of the tosylhydrazone. On heating, toluenesulfinate leaves and a diazo compound forms. In an aprotic solvent it loses N₂ to give a carbene, which undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the alkene. In a protic solvent the diazo carbon is protonated instead, N₂ leaves from the diazonium ion and a carbocation forms; that route gives more substituted alkenes and cation rearrangements.",
  "references": [
    "Clayden, Greeves and Warren, Organic Chemistry, 2nd edition (Oxford University Press), chapter 38"
